    94	
    95	## 2. Engine v2 (L0)
    96	
    97	**E1. Duties and keys.** `Auth = seat (ℓ : Label) (i : Fin (seats ℓ)) | none`.
    98	A proposal duty is a one-seat label. `act st ℓ` produces every duty
    99	payload. `ProtocolSpec` has no global `n`.
   100	
   101	**E2. Seats and assignment.** `seats : Label → Nat`,
   102	`Run.seatOf ℓ : Fin (seats ℓ) → V`. Committee models are L1 conditions:
   103	`CStar F` (seats = |V| and a proved `Fin (seats ℓ) ≃ V`), `Linked F` (one
   104	fixed bijection for all labels in F), `SingleSeat F`, and later
   105	`Weighted w F`, `Balanced ε F`. Honest code never sees keys. The
   106	protocol declares its AP label family `APLabels` and its FG label family
   107	explicitly (E5).
   108	
   109	**E3. Weights** are additions (seat multiplicity plus conditions).
   110	
   111	**E4. Delivery outcomes and deliverability (audit V01).**
   112	A delivery of `o` calls the handler; for each `o'` with `handles o o'`,
   113	the protocol classifies the call's effect on `o'` as one of:
   114	- *accepted*: `processed o'` becomes true;
   115	- *permanently rejected*: `excludes o'` holds from then on;
   116	- *temporarily refused*: the state does not change for `o'`, and
   117	  `excludes o'` is false (for example Grid CO's old HEAD after its
   118	  admission window; SA-PC's PC before `pcEnvelopeReady`; a future-label
   119	  faulty HEAD; a third equivocation).
   120	
   121	The network conditions are about handler calls, as today. Temporary
   122	refusal is allowed and needs no flag. Obligations (`HooksSound P`):
   123	- `excludes` is stable under `clock`, `process`, `recordOwn` and
   124	  `restore`, and implies that `process` leaves the state unchanged;
   125	- `processed` and `depsPresent` are monotone (`processed` may be derived);
   126	- `handles o o'` implies `o = o' ∨ carries o o'`.
   127	
   128	Deliverability (`Deliverable P`): every dependency of an object that an
   129	honest node emits was ACCEPTED by that node before the emission (it may
   130	be a faulty object), and the node's acceptance order is a finite
   131	delivery plan for it. `Kit.Engine` proves the run-extension theorem:
   132	under `HooksSound`, `Deliverable` and the relay premise, every valid
   133	execution prefix in a regime extends by the canonical synchronous
   134	scheduler to any horizon. This gives continuation feasibility from every
   135	accepted input, not only one long benign run.
   136	
   137	Two limits (async review):
   138	- Temporary refusal and synchrony. If the only delivery within Δ is
   139	  temporarily refused, the handler-call premise is met but the object is
   140	  not processed. A proof may assume later processing only if (a) the
   141	  regime includes the optional L1 condition `ReOffer` (the object is
   142	  delivered again within Δ after the first time it is no longer refused,
   143	  after GST), or (b) the protocol buffers the object in its state and
   144	  processes it later, or (c) the object reaches acceptance through the
   145	  relay premise (today's SA-PC). Each instance states which.
   146	- The run-extension theorem guarantees handler calls on the delivery
   147	  plan, not acceptance. A receiver may permanently reject an object that
   148	  the emitter accepted (for example FG admission with a different F,
   149	  possible only at a > f); excluded objects carry no obligation.
   150	
   151	These are NEW eligibility obligations. They are proved per migrated
   152	instance; they are not silently added to the v1 hooks (V07, M03).
   153	
   154	**E5. Own-signature record.**
   155	- `selfVisible : Label → Bool`, `recordOwn : State → Object → State`.
   156	- `World.step` takes the run context (seat assignment and `awake`).
   157	- Atomic tick: `clock`; every due payload from that post-clock state;
   158	  signing; `recordOwn` folded over self-visible objects in label order,
   159	  then seat order; release.
   160	- Separation: every label in `APLabels`, and every label in a `CStar`
   161	  family, has `selfVisible = false`.
   162	- Isolation laws, with `base : State → B`:
   163	  - `base` after `clock` and `process` is a function of `base` before and
   164	    of the time or object; `recordOwn` does not change `base`;
   165	  - `act` on `APLabels`, all gossip and every output getter factor
   166	    through `base`;
   167	  - restore law: `base (restore d)` depends only on the base-visible part
   168	    of `d` (for an FG composite: F and its basis), never on record-only
   169	    data (audit V11: otherwise a restart could copy a self-visible seat
   170	    index into the base);
   171	  - hook projection: `wellFormed`, `depsPresent`, `processed`,
   172	    `excludes` and `handles` restricted to AP objects factor through
   173	    `base`, so delivery regimes transport between a composite and its AP
   174	    projection.
   175	
   176	**E6. Restart.** `Event.restart v t`; order at one time: restart, ticks,
   177	deliveries. Step: `st ↦ restore (durable st)`. For an FG composite,
   178	`durable` = Λ + F + the F basis; h_max and J are derived again from
   179	received chain states. Each restart starts a new EPOCH of the node.
   180	Output monotonicity and inclusion compare reads of one node only within
   181	one epoch; a separate optional entry `catchUp` states that a rejoined
   182	node reaches its last exposed output within a bound (V11). Existing
   183	regimes add `NoRestart`.
   184	
   185	**Lift (v1 → v2).** A simulation, not only equal reads: disjoint label
   186	sum; `proposer s` ↦ seat 0 of `prop s`; `proposerOf s` = that seat's
   187	holder; old committee bijections from `CStar`'s proved equivalence (with
   188	the dependent `Fin` casts); old duty and proposal order preserved; no
   189	self-visible labels, `NoRestart`, trivial `durable`. Every v2 run in the
   190	lifted legacy regime has a corresponding v1 run. The lift transports
   191	claim PROPOSITIONS; certificate eligibility (E4, §5) is proved
   192	separately per instance. Estimate: several engineer-weeks (audit).
