   239	```
   240	
   241	- Per-parameter behaviour (audit tables): the claim is weaker when θ ↑,
   242	  θrec ↑, η ↑, A ↑, A_f ↑, ρ ↑, recoveryFull false → true, G ↓, Π ↓,
   243	  faultCap ↓, and D ↑ for persistent inclusion. Exact-time inclusion is
   244	  `fixed` in D: its `le` is equality on D.
   245	- `transport` is a proof, not metadata. Composite comparisons compose
   246	  transports; `le_refl` and `le_trans` make that sound.
   247	- Relation lemmas may combine certificates of ONE instance and require
   248	  equal values for their shared constants. Example:
   249	  `Safe o ∧ included o D ⇒ includedPersist o D` (same D). `Safe o` is one
   250	  conjunction: agreement and per-node monotonicity, as today's `SafeFrom`.
   251	- `includedPersist o D`: the honest proposal exists, and B is in every
   252	  honest read of `o` (all honest nodes, as `InBy`) at every time from
   253	  `s + D` to the horizon.
   254	- Regimes with a minimum horizon (Outage b₁, Recovery GST + ρ, StrongArmed
   255	  b₀) are closed under complete time truncation only above it. Causal
   256	  arguments use separate EXECUTION-PREFIX predicates (L1), not the
   257	  complete-run regimes; an event-list prefix inside one time can break
   258	  `tick_total`.
   259	
   260	**Profiles.** A profile is a set of catalog ids plus shared constant
   261	names. A profile certificate is a set of certificates of one instance
   262	that agree on the shared constants. `ac-4.3` is today's bundle: the
   263	equivalence is between claim PROPOSITIONS (`AvailableChain ↔` the
   264	conjunction of the `ac-4.3` `holds`). Eligibility (hooks,
   265	deliverability, inhabitation) is separate, because `AvailableChain`
   266	contains none of it (V07). `strong-1` is `StrongClaims`.
   267	
   268	Tiers:
   269	
   270	```text
   271	┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   272	│ Tier         │ Entries                                               │
   273	├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   274	│ core         │ Safe(confirmed), Safe(stable), prefix(stable ⪯        │
   275	│              │ confirmed), includedPersist(confirmed)                │
   276	│ standard     │ includedPersist(stable) under StableSleepy, fast      │
   277	│              │ inclusion, persistence (general, fast), recovered     │
   278	│              │ variants                                              │
   279	│ extra        │ strong claims; base exports; P1 from a recovered      │
   280	│              │ handoff; noFutureRead; catchUp; later additions       │
   281	│ fg-ready     │ AP-side FG entries (FG-COMPOSITION.md)                │
   282	│ composite    │ derived by Kit.Compose (FG-COMPOSITION.md)            │
   283	└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   284	```
   285	
   286	The core tier is a user decision.
   287	
   288	## 5. Certificates, refutations, registry (L4, L6)
   289	
   290	```lean
   291	structure Cert (c : ClaimSpec) (I : Instance) where
   292	  const     : c.Const
   293	  valid     : c.valid const
   294	  inhabited : c.inhabited I const
   295	  proof     : c.holds I const
   296	
   297	structure Refutation (c : ClaimSpec) (I : Instance) where
   298	  const     : c.Const
   299	  valid     : c.valid const
   300	  proof     : ¬ c.holds I const
   301	```
   302	
   303	**Inhabitation (V07).** `inhabited I c`: for every horizon H there is a
   304	run of I in the claim's regime, with horizon ≥ H, in which the claim's
   305	TRIGGER occurs with its deadline inside the run: for inclusion, an
   306	honest awake proposer slot; for persistence, a non-genesis armed read;
   307	for FG entries, a nonempty source. Boundary cases are allowed (no fault
   308	when the regime forbids one). `Kit.Engine` supplies the runs through the
   309	run-extension theorem (E4). Transport of `holds` does not transport
   310	`inhabited`.
   311	
   312	**Refutations.** Finite runs for bounded claims; parameterized finite
   313	families for "no constant works" (as the Sanity theorem does for every
   314	D). Kernel checking with `decide_cbv` or a reflective checker whose
   315	soundness covers the execution, the regime and the failed conclusion;
   316	`native_decide` is not allowed. Executable checkers exist only for
   317	instances with finite representations and equivalence proofs.
   318	
   319	**Registry.** Checked attributes (`@[cert]`, `@[refutes]`) verify the
   320	elaborated type, including instance and constants; a theorem with extra
   321	unproved premises is not a certificate. Per instance and claim the
   322	registry keeps the Pareto set of proved constants, the refuted
   323	constants, the proof status (Lean certificate, paper, conditional),
   324	assumptions, output names and module versions. A paper result never
   325	appears as a Lean certificate.
   326	
   327	**Freeze.** `catalog.lock`: per L1, LM, L2, L3 declaration, a
   328	deterministic hash of the transitive dependency closure (types, bodies,
   329	inductives, opaque bodies via `getUsedConstantsAsSet`; fail if a body is
   330	unavailable). Per L0 and LM version, a frozen content hash. The
   331	toolchain and Lake manifest are pinned. Each certificate records its
   332	instance, constants and dependency hashes. The freeze is syntactic; it
   333	does not replace kernel checking.
   334	
   335	## 6. Kits and explorer (L5, L6)
   336	
   337	- `Kit.Engine`: honest origin; transfer within Δ after GST and on a
   338	  healthy prefix; relay closure; clean start; the run-extension theorem
   339	  and inhabitation (E4, §5).
   340	- `Kit.Goldfish`: the substrate of Grid CO, SA, SA-PC and L*. Optional.
   341	- `Kit.FG`, `Kit.Compose`: see §7 and FG-COMPOSITION.md.
   342	- Explorer: executable instances; regime and conclusion checkers;
   343	  refutation files.
   344	
   345	## 7. FG composition: interface shapes
   346	
   347	The exact FG statements, their proof status and their sources are in
   348	`docs/FG-COMPOSITION.md`. They follow the research and enter the catalog
   349	as versioned entries. This section fixes only the shapes that the
   350	framework must support.
   351	
   352	**7.1 Decisions (user).** The FG is fixed (our Simplex FG; LM
   353	definitions, `Kit.FG` proofs). APs vary. FG heights never skip. Every
   354	height pair (target or timeout) needs the AP's source; with none, the
   355	pair is empty. FG interference is relative to a height m. Fallback: an AP
   356	that cannot meet the AP-side entries is certified white-box.
   357	
   358	**7.2 Duty frame (LM; audit V09).** `frameOf : View → Frame` returns the
   359	duty frame (F_d, Z_d, H_d, (j_d, J_d), D_d) and the view V_d, with
   360	D_d = {B ∈ V_d : F_d ⪯ B, Z_d ⪯ B, ∃ W ∈ V_d, B ⪯ W ∧ h(W) ≥ H_d − 1}
   361	and Z_d = J_d if H_d = j_d + 1, else F_d. The view is the duty's frozen
   362	view closed under the F basis. This is the R1 VARIANT of DC: DC reads
   363	live control caches; R1 reads the duty view. Two correspondence theorems
   364	(versioned, in `Kit.FG`) relate the frame to the R1 variant of DC's
   365	viable tree and to its filtered tree, separately (the root covers the
   366	prefix up to Z_d). `anchors` (the entries at H_d − 1 in D_d) are a derived
   367	view of the frame; the AP may pick among them with its `AnchorChoice`.
   368	
   369	**7.3 AP template and policy type (LM; audit V10).**
   370	- `Policy`: the type of honest FG signing rules. A policy reads the FG
   371	  sub-state, the record, and the AP facets through `base`; it emits
   372	  objects only on the FG label family (`Linked`); it writes only the
   373	  record. The AP transitions are fixed.
   374	- `APTemplate`: an AP spec with a policy slot. `inst T σ : Instance` is
   375	  the composite for policy σ.
   376	- An AP-side certificate is a FAMILY: `∀ σ : Policy, Cert c (inst T σ)`,
   377	  proved once, uniformly. Its proof may use only the frame conditions of
   378	  FG-COMPOSITION.md and AP facts. Simplex laws (provenance, quorum
   379	  uniqueness, causal cap, no slashing) are used only after
   380	  specialization to the Simplex policy.
   381	- Conditional regimes state their quantifier order: inhabitation is
   382	  required for the Simplex policy, not for every σ.
   383	
   384	**7.4 Composition (`Kit.Compose`).** It combines AP-side certificate
   385	families, specialized to the Simplex policy, with `Kit.FG` lemmas, in
   386	the causal induction over execution prefixes, with separate invariants
   387	for clean history, post-reset source history (per-body first-transfer
   388	time), and armed-prefix Outage history (V03). Its outputs are the
   389	composite entries of FG-COMPOSITION.md (`comp.fgHealed`, `comp.healed`,
   390	and finality entries that stay conditional until their timed
   391	obligations are proved).
   392	
