   154	
   155	**V01 — E4 cannot be proved for the existing handlers.** Deliver an unseen old Grid CO HEAD after its admission window. `wellFormed` and `depsPresent` are true, and `handles o o` holds. The handler leaves the state unchanged, `processed o` remains false, and `excludes` is always false. All three outcomes allowed by the new handler law therefore fail. SA uses this same handler. SA-PC gives another example: a PC can have its paths present but fail the slot test in `pcEnvelopeReady`. A future-label faulty HEAD and a third equivocation also expose this distinction between a handler call and acceptance. These are protocol behaviours, not merely malformed Lean states. [Grid CO handler](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:300), [Grid CO hooks](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:344), [SA-PC handler](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainSAPC/Model/Handlers.lean:45).
   156	
   157	The deliverability clause has two further problems. An honest proposal can extend a previously accepted **faulty** proposal; its dependency need not have an honest emission origin. Also, dependencies emitted at equal times can form a cycle. Their emission does not establish an order in which the receiver can accept them. Use accepted dependency provenance, a finite delivery plan, and explicit readiness conditions. Do not strengthen the old hooks silently and then claim unchanged certificate transport. [Proposer code](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:287), [E4](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:175).
   158	
   159	**V02 — `Retains` needs both the correct predicate and the correct time boundary.** An anchor comparable with B does not imply that B is present in the duty view. With anchor genesis, every possible B is comparable, including an unseen block. Nor does it imply that B belongs to the allowed tree: B can lie strictly below the current root. The correct predicate permits that case because the root already contains B:
   160	
   161	`B ⪯ Z_d ∨ B ∈ D_d`.
   162	
   163	For a fresh source selected at action `a_r=d_s`, the proof also needs this predicate at `q_s=a_r−Δ`. v5 does not define `u_B` to supply that boundary. Setting it to the action time is too late. Explicit X prevents an empty-set exception only when X is nonempty; it does not fix either defect. r147 states both requirements directly. [Ret predicate](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:117), [source boundary](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:140).
   164	
   165	**V03 — The source-body induction needs stronger AP facts and narrower scope.** The referenced I1–I3 supply source possession, same-round compatibility, and later coverage of an **entry** T. They do not by themselves give later coverage of the whole source body C. Two bodies can conflict above the same entry T while both satisfy entry-level conclusions. Similarly, an arbitrary `Protected` getter does not establish that the supporter's actual HEAD contained the source. Sharing the getter between the two sides removes inconsistent meanings; it does not create the required semantic law. [I1–I3](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/dc-recovery-map.md:60), [AP entries](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:513).
   166	
   167	The invariant at D:570 also needs a reset boundary and a per-body first-transfer time. It cannot retain all pre-reset sources: old-justification disclosure is a documented counterexample. During Outage, two partition branches can both extend an armed B; P1 protects B without ordering all later bodies. Thus the unqualified same-round compatibility clause and the global source-chain invariant cannot serve all listed regimes. The proof needs separate clean history, post-reset source history, and armed-prefix Outage history. `Harmless` must use compatibility with the body being protected, not just provenance of one row in the new quorum. [Actual history invariant](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:417), [Outage proof](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:366), [old-disclosure counterexample](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-fast.md:553).
   168	
   169	**V04 — Height-relative interference does not mean permanent retention of every high block.** The user's finalized-height rule is sound in its stated role: under the capped Simplex certificate assumptions, every valid block above `h*` extends F*, so disclosure of J at `h*` cannot remove such a block by selecting another predecessor branch.
   170	
   171	But D:554 says more. Let two valid branches extend F* and reach height `m=h*+1`. Honest progress continues on one branch. Once its frontier reaches `m+2`, a block B left on the other branch at height m can have no descendant witness at height `m+1`. The height filter removes B. This is consistent with finality safety and with B extending F*. NJ likewise does not keep every unused branch forever. The supported conclusion concerns old control evidence and the specified compatible source history. It does not concern every processed block above m. [DC viability](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:911), [finalized-arm proof](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:495).
   172	
   173	**V05 — The nine-step recovery plan still has open interfaces.** `ap.includesRows` needs an eligible-row window, a cutoff tied to the action time, and the alternative “already folded on the parent chain.” A cutoff with no lower bound can omit every useful row while satisfying the stated inclusion rule. Conversely, requiring every previously received row is stronger than DC's bounded inclusion rule. Steps 4–5 also need the selected carrier chain to keep the matching entry until consumption, or to have already achieved the required height rise. r147 proves these facts with explicit proposal times, maturity, parent-chain storage and two promotions. [Carrier construction](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:485).
   174	
   175	Adding `ap.outputRecovered` is a valid way to expose an obligation, but it does not discharge it for FG-SA. Row-source retention alone does not cover qualifying fast bodies at every label, slow-source windows, or exact proposal-parent service. The new blueprint marks that composition gap OPEN. First finality still needs the permitted finality record, a usable non-NJ height, and timed target/justification/finality carriers. `ap.finalityHead` supplies only part of this. Keep these conclusions conditional until the required AP statements and FG constructions are complete. [Full output handoff](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:635), [remaining finality obligations](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:670), [blueprint interface gap](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-healing-blueprint.md:964).
   176	
   177	**V06 — `Settled` is neither the proved static condition nor the proved effective condition.** A union of anchor sets can be an antichain while individual sets shrink or alternate. For example, `{X}` and `{Y}`, with X and Y incomparable, meet that test. The predicate also does not state a common numerical frontier H. Its following sentence assumes “the band fixed,” but that assumption is absent from the definition. r147 instead fixes H, F and Z and stops the argument at the first height rise. [v5 condition](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:486), [r147 N1](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:127).
   178	
   179	Literal F constancy creates a separate bound issue. There is at most one branch-excluding top-J event at a fixed frontier, but there can be many lower F advances. Counting each as a new repair attempt gives an initial-frontier-dependent bound. The uniform bound requires a proof that exact service continues through harmless lower F advances. The blueprint marks that stronger interface SKETCH. v5 must select one form and its corresponding bound. [r147 count](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:597), [bound consequence](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-healing-blueprint.md:887).
   180	
   181	**V07 — Arbitrary-horizon inhabitation is useful but does not prove meaningful coverage.** A witness can deliver heartbeats forever while having no eligible proposal, no non-genesis armed output, or no nonempty FG source. A protocol can also have long benign runs while a particular accepted input makes future synchrony impossible. Existential witnesses for each horizon do not prove continuation from that input. Witness obligations should be claim-specific and exercise the trigger and deadline; scheduler feasibility needs its own stated scope. [Witness requirement](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:359).
   182	
   183	The promised equivalence to the old bundle must also be qualified. `AvailableChain` contains neither an emission witness nor `HooksSound`. For example, a v1 specification can schedule every proposal at a nonpositive time and emit nothing. Its clean inclusion triggers can be empty, while its genesis outputs satisfy safety. The new emission witness cannot follow from that bundle. Prove equivalence between the **claim propositions**, then prove certification eligibility separately for each migrated instance. [Old bundle](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Claims.lean:23), [inclusion trigger](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Properties.lean:89).
   184	
   185	**V08 — Claim bounds must not be part of the fixed protocol identity.** With `I.params` fixed, one I cannot have two certified delay/budget tradeoffs. Creating a new Instance for each tuple restores representability but defeats the stated “per instance” Pareto set and requires a separate notion of equal protocol and facets. Keep protocol parameters and facets fixed; bind theorem bounds in certificates and coherent profiles. The project log already plans this correction, but v5 has not applied it. [Instance schema](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:235), [planned correction](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/log.md:78).
   186	
   187	Moreover, v5 has removed the explicit `Prem`, `Concl`, `monoPrem` and `monoConcl` interface. `dir` alone does not replace it. A checked transport must keep the protocol and facets fixed, check parameter validity, and prove `holds p → holds p'`. Exact-time D needs equality-only comparison unless a separate monotonicity certificate is supplied. Composite comparisons need reflexivity/transitivity or equivalent checked entailment composition. Inhabitation must remain a separate obligation.
   188	
   189	**V09 — The anchor correspondence names the wrong set and loses a control value.** If root = J and `F ≺ J`, F can still belong to DC's viable tree. It does not belong to paths that start at J. Thus the equality at D:459 cannot literally describe `viable_tree_from`; it describes a representation of the **filtered** tree, with separate treatment of prefixes already covered by the root. [Viable tree](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:911), [filtered tree](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:950).
   190	
   191	Also, `(F,{A})` does not identify whether the root is F or A: A can be the only band entry, or the active J. `Settled` nevertheless refers to that root. Return a frame containing the accepted F, Z, H and the duty view, or prove an explicit reconstruction theorem. The F basis, frozen branches and F-advance cut are correct ingredients. R1 remains a deliberate variant of DC, so the correspondence must name that variant rather than imply executable equivalence to live-cache DC.
   192	
   193	**V10 — Universal Havoc policies need a separate typed proof boundary.** Quantification over σ is not inherently unsound. It is a useful robustness test. But σ must have a defined type and access boundary: which state it reads, which rows it emits, what record updates it can cause, and which AP transitions remain fixed. Each composite has a different `ProtocolSpec`; the certificate therefore needs an indexed instance/facet family or a separate AP-template certificate.
   194	
   195	The cited r147 proof assumes the specified FG-SA client and honest non-equivocation. It cannot simply be reused as a proof for every equivocal σ. AP results must be proved using only the permitted filter conditions and AP facts. Simplex provenance, quorum uniqueness, causal-cap and no-slashing lemmas must be used **after** specialization. Conditional-regime inhabitation also needs an explicit quantifier order; a useful `Settled` interval need not exist under every policy. [Havoc definition](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:472), [r147 execution premises](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:36).
   196	
   197	**V11 — Restart can bypass the proposed isolation law.** Consider a hidden record field containing a self-visible seat index. Let `recordOwn` change only that field, while all ordinary AP transitions and getters factor through `base`. Let `restore (durable st)` copy the hidden index into `base`. This satisfies the listed E5 equations, yet an AP proposal after restart can reveal and use the index. A restore projection law is required. Hook predicates also need a stated projection contract if the framework will transport delivery regimes between the composite and its AP projection. [Isolation and restart](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:195).
   198	
   199	Use an explicit AP-label family for isolation. “Every C* label” is ambiguous because full-set Linked labels also have bijective seat assignments. Finally, excluding the rejoin interval does not define cross-restart monotonicity: a read before restart and a read after rejoin can both lie outside the excluded interval and still show a retreat. Use epoch-local comparisons or require catch-up to the last exposed output. Define the record fold order over both duties and seats.
