    80	
    81	**B01. Certificate meanings must be shared.** `Cert` chooses its own facets. A profile is only a set of claim IDs. Thus one protocol can certify safety using an output that always returns genesis, then certify inclusion using a different getter under the same output name. For example, an output can show each of two conflicting honest proposals at its inclusion deadline. It can satisfy inclusion while failing safety; the genesis getter supplies the separate safety certificate. The current bundle instead uses one `I` and one `C` throughout. Profiles need a common instance interface, consistent parameter bindings, and explicit facet equality or refinement proofs. Cross-claim inequalities alone do not fix this. [Design:344](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:344), [current bundle:23](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Claims.lean:23).
    82	
    83	**B02. Delay weakening is false for independent inclusion claims.** `IncludedFrom … D` requires containment at exactly `proposalTime + D`. It does not require containment at later reads. An output can contain B at `s + D`, then remove B before `s + D′`. This refutes the proposed generic implication for `D ≤ D′`. All current inclusion fields have this problem when separated from their safety fields. The complete bundles can supply monotonicity, but partial certification cannot assume that other certificate. Preserve the old exact-delay entry and use a relation theorem that requires the same output’s monotonicity certificate. Alternatively, introduce a new entry with persistent inclusion. [Properties:89](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Properties.lean:89), [design:286](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:286).
    84	
    85	**B03. One witness does not prevent vacuity.** Consider a protocol that emits a valid heartbeat and an undeliverable object at time zero. Give the second object `depsPresent = false`, `excludes = false`, and no alternative handler. These hooks satisfy the listed monotonicity obligations. A short run can deliver the heartbeat and satisfy the proposed witness. However, once the broadcast deadline enters the horizon, synchrony requires a delivery that `DeliveryWellFormed` forbids. No longer synchronous run exists. Future inclusion obligations can therefore hold vacuously. The witness does not even depend on the conclusion delay. Require semantic links between hooks and execution, honest-emission admissibility, and feasibility beyond each relevant deadline—preferably a run-extension theorem. Also, `handles → equality ∨ carries` proves containment, not that the handler actually ran. [Design:133](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:133), [delivery checks:51](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Conditions.lean:51), [broadcast:110](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Conditions.lean:110).
    86	
    87	**B04. The anchor predicates need a new statement.** Under the natural compatibility interpretation, `Static` permits arbitrary anchor changes while there are no honest targets: its exception is then vacuous. HavocFG can keep changing the effective choice domain before a source forms. This is not the fixed-domain interval used by the research repair proof. Conversely, literal equality of anchor sets can be too strong: new branches can enter a view without changing the effective root or numerical band. Define the effective controls and the retained paths, with explicit interval endpoints, settling bounds, and quantifiers over duties. Define HavocFG as a family indexed by a signing rule; the AP facets and certificates must be uniform across that family. [Design:481](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:481), [design:501](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:501), [fixed-frontier proof:513](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:513).
    88	
    89	The Outage statement has a separate error. Persistence of an armed B does not imply that all later honest leases and stable tips remain on one chain. During a partition, nodes can adopt conflicting descendants X and Y of B while both retain B. The research proof protects **B**, using a first departure from B. It does not prove compatibility of every new protected descendant. Consequently, (C) cannot generally maintain the proposed global `ProtectedCompatible` predicate throughout Outage. Use a predicate indexed by the protected read or armed prefix. State same-round target compatibility only in regimes that establish it. [Design:507](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:507), [Outage proof:380](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:380).
    90	
