   155	**V01 — E4 cannot be proved for the existing handlers.** Deliver an unseen old Grid CO HEAD after its admission window. `wellFormed` and `depsPresent` are true, and `handles o o` holds. The handler leaves the state unchanged, `processed o` remains false, and `excludes` is always false. All three outcomes allowed by the new handler law therefore fail. SA uses this same handler. SA-PC gives another example: a PC can have its paths present but fail the slot test in `pcEnvelopeReady`. A future-label faulty HEAD and a third equivocation also expose this distinction between a handler call and acceptance. These are protocol behaviours, not merely malformed Lean states. [Grid CO handler](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:300), [Grid CO hooks](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:344), [SA-PC handler](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainSAPC/Model/Handlers.lean:45).
   156	
   157	The deliverability clause has two further problems. An honest proposal can extend a previously accepted **faulty** proposal; its dependency need not have an honest emission origin. Also, dependencies emitted at equal times can form a cycle. Their emission does not establish an order in which the receiver can accept them. Use accepted dependency provenance, a finite delivery plan, and explicit readiness conditions. Do not strengthen the old hooks silently and then claim unchanged certificate transport. [Proposer code](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainGridCO/Model/Protocol.lean:287), [E4](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:175).
   158	
   159	**V02 — `Retains` needs both the correct predicate and the correct time boundary.** An anchor comparable with B does not imply that B is present in the duty view. With anchor genesis, every possible B is comparable, including an unseen block. Nor does it imply that B belongs to the allowed tree: B can lie strictly below the current root. The correct predicate permits that case because the root already contains B:
   160	
   161	`B ⪯ Z_d ∨ B ∈ D_d`.
   162	
   163	For a fresh source selected at action `a_r=d_s`, the proof also needs this predicate at `q_s=a_r−Δ`. v5 does not define `u_B` to supply that boundary. Setting it to the action time is too late. Explicit X prevents an empty-set exception only when X is nonempty; it does not fix either defect. r147 states both requirements directly. [Ret predicate](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:117), [source boundary](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:140).
   164	
   165	**V03 — The source-body induction needs stronger AP facts and narrower scope.** The referenced I1–I3 supply source possession, same-round compatibility, and later coverage of an **entry** T. They do not by themselves give later coverage of the whole source body C. Two bodies can conflict above the same entry T while both satisfy entry-level conclusions. Similarly, an arbitrary `Protected` getter does not establish that the supporter's actual HEAD contained the source. Sharing the getter between the two sides removes inconsistent meanings; it does not create the required semantic law. [I1–I3](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/dc-recovery-map.md:60), [AP entries](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:513).
   166	
   167	The invariant at D:570 also needs a reset boundary and a per-body first-transfer time. It cannot retain all pre-reset sources: old-justification disclosure is a documented counterexample. During Outage, two partition branches can both extend an armed B; P1 protects B without ordering all later bodies. Thus the unqualified same-round compatibility clause and the global source-chain invariant cannot serve all listed regimes. The proof needs separate clean history, post-reset source history, and armed-prefix Outage history. `Harmless` must use compatibility with the body being protected, not just provenance of one row in the new quorum. [Actual history invariant](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:417), [Outage proof](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:366), [old-disclosure counterexample](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-fast.md:553).
   168	
   169	**V04 — Height-relative interference does not mean permanent retention of every high block.** The user's finalized-height rule is sound in its stated role: under the capped Simplex certificate assumptions, every valid block above `h*` extends F*, so disclosure of J at `h*` cannot remove such a block by selecting another predecessor branch.
   170	
   171	But D:554 says more. Let two valid branches extend F* and reach height `m=h*+1`. Honest progress continues on one branch. Once its frontier reaches `m+2`, a block B left on the other branch at height m can have no descendant witness at height `m+1`. The height filter removes B. This is consistent with finality safety and with B extending F*. NJ likewise does not keep every unused branch forever. The supported conclusion concerns old control evidence and the specified compatible source history. It does not concern every processed block above m. [DC viability](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:911), [finalized-arm proof](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:495).
