   173	**V05 — The nine-step recovery plan still has open interfaces.** `ap.includesRows` needs an eligible-row window, a cutoff tied to the action time, and the alternative “already folded on the parent chain.” A cutoff with no lower bound can omit every useful row while satisfying the stated inclusion rule. Conversely, requiring every previously received row is stronger than DC's bounded inclusion rule. Steps 4–5 also need the selected carrier chain to keep the matching entry until consumption, or to have already achieved the required height rise. r147 proves these facts with explicit proposal times, maturity, parent-chain storage and two promotions. [Carrier construction](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-conditional.md:485).
   174	
   175	Adding `ap.outputRecovered` is a valid way to expose an obligation, but it does not discharge it for FG-SA. Row-source retention alone does not cover qualifying fast bodies at every label, slow-source windows, or exact proposal-parent service. The new blueprint marks that composition gap OPEN. First finality still needs the permitted finality record, a usable non-NJ height, and timed target/justification/finality carriers. `ap.finalityHead` supplies only part of this. Keep these conclusions conditional until the required AP statements and FG constructions are complete. [Full output handoff](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:635), [remaining finality obligations](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:670), [blueprint interface gap](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-healing-blueprint.md:964).
