   103	## 2. Two precise meanings of no FG interference
   104	
   105	For each honest duty d, take its own R1 view V_d. Compute its controls
   106	together, as in the rule documents:
   107	
   108	```text
   109	F_d = current accepted F, with its retained acceptance basis;
   110	H_d = max({1} ∪ {h(W) : W∈V_d});
   111	(j_d,J_d) = greatest justified pair in V_d compatible with F_d;
   112	Z_d = J_d if H_d=j_d+1, else F_d;
   113	
   114	D_d = {B∈V_d : F_d⪯B, Z_d⪯B,
   115	                  ∃W∈V_d, B⪯W and h(W)≥H_d−1}.
   116	
   117	Ret_d(B) := B⪯Z_d
   118	            or (Z_d⪯B and B∈D_d).
   119	```
   120	
   121	The first arm of Ret allows the root to move past B along B's chain.
   122	It is therefore weaker than requiring B to remain a member of D_d.
   123	The second arm requires a band witness in **this** view. Compatibility
   124	with F and Z alone is insufficient. Ancestor closure supplies the path
   125	from Z_d to B in this arm.
   126	
   127	**N1(u,v;m,F,Z): static controls.** At every honest proposal, HEAD, and
   128	FG choice duty in `[u,v]`, `H_d=m`, `F_d=F`, and `Z_d=Z`. R1 and the
   129	retained-F-basis rule continue to hold. Each duty computes D_d from its
   130	own view. The full archival honest frontier is m until the stopping
   131	event under consideration. Views, block sets, scores, leases, and raw
   132	results need not be equal. New blocks and band witnesses may arrive.
   133	The unused lower justified pair need not be fixed when Z=F.
   134	
   135	N1 concerns the selection rule, not literal equality of D_d. This matters:
   136	the honest proposal itself must enter later views. A static filter may
   137	also exclude an old lease target below the band. No claim that all old
   138	protected blocks remain eligible is part of N1.
   139	
   140	**N2(C,s;a_r,t): preservation for one source.** Let `s=rR` and
   141	`a_r=d_s`. Require Ret_d(C) at every honest transfer HEAD at q_s, and
   142	at every subsequent honest choice duty through t. Equivalently this is
   143	preservation on `[q_s,t]`, with the first transfer HEAD included. This
   144	condition refers only to F_d, Z_d, H_d, V_d, and their domain predicate;
   145	it does not assert that any actual HEAD or S already contains C.
   146	
   147	For a family of rows, **N2(u,t)** means N2(C,s;a_r,t) for each honest
   148	nonempty height row with `a_r∈[u,t]` and its selected fresh source C.
   149	This definition includes the source boundary of each row. In particular,
   150	the first row's q_s can be one Δ before u.
   151	
   152	This boundary is essential to the statement being proved. A condition
   153	only on duties whose wall-clock times are in `[a_r,t]` does not supply a
   154	premise about the already completed HEAD at `q_s=a_r−Δ`. When this file
   155	says “N2 on `[a_r,t]`”, it means the explicit source-closed definition
   156	above. It does not silently infer that earlier HEAD from future controls.
   157	An alternative interface is Ret at q_s as a separate premise, followed
   158	by ordinary forward preservation on `[a_r,t]`.
   159	
   160	These conditions serve different jobs. At fixed height, finite evidence
   161	changes imply N1 after relay and freeze settling (§6). At NJ+1, the other
   162	job must establish N2, including its first q_s boundary, from the causal
   163	cap, the NJ records, and source history. N2 allows later height changes,
   164	new compatible F, and roots above C; it does not require a static band
   165	after NJ. Neither definition assumes base recovery or the desired HEAD
   166	conclusion.
