   784	### 7.9 Height-relative noninterference: one form for both arms
   785	
   786	This section answers the user input of 2026-09-28: if H is finalized,
   787	possibly without honest nodes knowing it, then no FG interference can
   788	occur at H+1, although FG values can still change.
   789	
   790	**Definition (HNI).** For a height m ≥ 1, *height-m noninterference*,
   791	HNI(m), holds iff, over the whole run:
   792	
   793	- (h1) every justified entry at height m−1 precedes every valid block of
   794	  height ≥ m; and
   795	- (h2) every finalized checkpoint of height ≤ m−1 precedes every valid
   796	  block of height ≥ m.
   797	
   798	HNI(m) speaks only about FG objects **below** m. It says that they never
   799	separate blocks at height ≥ m. It does not say when an honest node learns
   800	of such an object. A justified entry at a height < m−1 needs no clause:
   801	it is never the root at a view with `H_d ≥ m`.
   802	
   803	**Lemma HNI-a — PROVED.** Assume HNI(m). At every honest duty d with
   804	`H_d = m`, every block B∈V_d with `h(B) ≥ m` is kept. This holds whatever
   805	F_d is and whether J at m−1 is known.
   806	
   807	Proof. At H_d = m, Z_d is J_d at m−1 or F_d with `h(F_d) ≤ m−1`. By (h1)
   808	or (h2), `Z_d ⪯ B`. The band threshold is m−1 ≤ h(B), so B is its own
   809	witness. Hence B∈D_d. ∎
   810	
   811	Thus, at frontier m under HNI(m), a change of root (a revealed J at m−1,
   812	or an F advance) prunes only blocks that do not reach height m. This is
   813	the user's statement, and it is PROVED.
   814	
   815	**Lemma HNI-b — PROVED.** Assume HNI(m). At every honest duty d with
   816	`H_d ≥ m`, either `h(Z_d) ≥ m`, or Z_d precedes every block of V_d of
   817	height ≥ m. In the first case Z_d is a justified entry at a height ≥ m.
   818	
   819	Proof. If `h(Z_d) ≤ m−1`, then Z_d is F_d (with h ≤ m−1) or a J at
   820	`H_d−1`. The second option forces `H_d = m` and J at m−1. Apply (h2) or
   821	(h1). ∎
   822	
   823	So the only FG objects that can remove a block of height ≥ m are
   824	justified entries at heights ≥ m, and band thresholds above m. Both are
   825	made from honest rows at heights ≥ m (F1, F11). This is the split that
   826	Lemma K uses. In Lemma K, (K2) with F3 and F4 is exactly HNI(x+1), and
   827	(K1) controls the objects at heights ≥ x+1.
   828	
   829	**The two arms satisfy HNI(x+1).**
   830	
   831	- *Finalized arm — PROVED.* x = hF0 and F* is finalized at x. (h1): every
   832	  justified entry at x equals F* (F3), and F* precedes every block of
   833	  height > x (F4). (h2): a finalized checkpoint of height ≤ x precedes
   834	  every block of height > its own height (F4). F4 is
   835	  [finalized_preceq_of_height_lt][dc-finality]. Its proof uses only the
   836	  existence of the finality quorum in the run and the honest record
   837	  order: a height crossing at x with another entry and the finality
   838	  quorum share an honest identity, and that identity's first signature
   839	  forbids the second, in either order. So HNI(x+1) holds for the whole
   840	  run, including crossings signed **before** the finality pairs, and
   841	  including honest nodes that sign rows at x+1 without knowing F*. The
   842	  revelation of F* at a view with H_d = x+1 moves the root to F*; by
   843	  HNI-a it prunes only blocks that never reach x+1.
   844	- *NJ arm — PROVED from (S).* x = H. (h2) is F4. (h1) holds vacuously
   845	  because no justified entry at H ever exists. In the DC prefix this is
   846	  F7. For the whole run it is Theorem N clause (ii) with F1 (N-b). The NJ
   847	  arm therefore needs an induction, and the finalized arm does not.
   848	
   849	**DC definition check.** In
   850	[HeightRegimeNamedBaseDeadline][dc-deadline],
   851	`exists_namedHeightRegimeBaseRun_of_eventualHeightProgress` takes `first`
   852	as the first index with frontier H+2, and `hF0 =
   853	recoveryPrefixFinalityHeight S rho first`, the greatest h_F of a body in
   854	an honest store before `first`. If `RecoveryHeight hF0 H` fails, it takes
   855	a body with `h_F = hF0`, its checkpoint F* (`namedFinalization_selfTarget_deadline`),
   856	`blocked = hF0 ≤ H+1`, and the first index `next` with frontier hF0+2.
   857	`namedHeightRegimeFrame_of_finalizedRun` then gives `floor`, `rootBelow`,
   858	and `sourceAbove` at blocked hF0 with `Tprev = F*`. Its `rootBelow` field
   859	is HNI-a restricted to blocks that extend F*. The user's case, where H
   860	itself is finalized, is `hF0 = H`; then m = H+1 in both arms. The DC
   861	definition requires only that some honest store holds the finalizing
   862	body before `first`. It does not require that the rows at hF0+1 come
   863	after that knowledge, which agrees with the timeless proof above.
   864	
   865	**Literal reading — REFUTED.** The statement "every later change of an
   866	honest anchor set or domain keeps every block of height ≥ m" is false
   867	after the frontier passes m. Witness, in any healthy stretch after the
   868	reset with `n=4, a=f=1, q_F=3`: at a round r the three honest rows
   869	target `(y,T)` with y ≥ x+1. The honest carrier Q at slot rR+2 folds them
   870	and enters y+1 with entry Q. A faulty proposer at slot rR+3 makes Y with
   871	the same parent as Q and the same three rows. Y also enters y+1 (any
   872	child can consume a quorum), with entry Y, and Y ⪯̸ Q. The honest rows at
   873	y+1 name Q, and the carrier of J_(y+1)=Q enters y+2. At every duty whose
   874	view contains that carrier, `H_d = y+2 = j_d+1`, so Z_d = Q. Y has
   875	height y+1 ≥ m and is lost. This is DC's `get_fg_root` rule. It is not
   876	interference: Y has no honest source, and every protected block stays
   877	kept (Theorem N). The correct statement is HNI-a (at H_d = m) together
   878	with Theorem N (for protected blocks at every later duty).
   879	
   880	**What HNI means for (S) and (L).**
   881	
   882	- (S) keeps its premise: B is kept. HNI(x+1) plus source compatibility
   883	  discharges it (Lemma K, Theorem N). A height-relative premise cannot
   884	  replace keep inside (S): above x+1, keep of B depends on honest rows at
   885	  heights ≥ x+1, and those rows are what (S) protects.
   886	- (L) keeps its static form after the one bullet. HNI(m) does not reduce
   887	  the count of §4.2, which is already one at every frontier. It only
   888	  makes that bullet harmless for blocks of height ≥ m. If the base chain
   889	  still has height m−1 when J_(m−1) appears, HNI permits its pruning,
   890	  and the case split still pays θ+λ once. If the base chain already has
   891	  height ≥ m, the bullet costs nothing. The bound of §9 is unchanged.
   892	- In the fixed-height case split, HNI(m) need not hold at a generic
   893	  frontier m. DC permits a timeout for entry T2 at m−1 after a target
   894	  for T1 at m−1, so a progress quorum for T2 and a justification of T1
   895	  can both exist. CE-1 of [fgsa-fast-opus §5.2](fgsa-fast-opus.md) has
   896	  this form: the post-GST rows `(2,Y)` (two timeouts, one target) can
   897	  make a Y carrier of height 3, and at frontier 3 the revealed root X
   898	  (J at 2) excludes it. So (h1) of HNI(3) fails there. NJ and finality
   899	  are the two ways to make every crossing of x use one entry, or no
   900	  justification at all. That is why the reset height must be NJ or
   901	  finalized.
   902	- The two losses on record ([fgsa-fast §8](fgsa-fast.md) at 214 and CE-1
   903	  at 50) remove blocks of height m−1 at frontier m (Y168 at 3, and Y9 at
   904	  2). HNI(m) permits that. What makes them harmful is that honest rows
   905	  at height m−1 named those blocks, and the excluding J at m−1 was made
   906	  from older honest rows on another branch. Above x, Theorem N excludes
   907	  exactly this: every row at a height ≥ x+1 has round ≥ r*, and all
   908	  their sources are compatible.
