   653	## 7. What is paid per interval, and limits of the claims
   654	
   655	The proved (L) bound pays ρ after the start of **each** static interval
   656	used for an attempt. The two-lease cutoff is relative to that start.
   657	This is an upper-bound choice, not a proof that each change necessarily
   658	costs ρ.
   659	
   660	Opus §6.5 instead uses one global post-GST two-lease cutoff. The cited
   661	SA theorem obtains supported live rows from **continuing** fast or
   662	slow source support. Its proof calls `fast_source_permanent_support`
   663	or `slow_source_permanent_support`, then `rows_supported_of_sources`
   664	[SA2, SA7, SA8]. Synchrony of an old source window does not supply the
   665	domain premise at later transfers after a root or band change. An
   666	excluded lease remains stored; its pending gate can write after the
   667	exclusion; another change can make that lease effective again.
   668	
   669	Thus “all surviving windows are synchronous” does not complete the
   670	proposed one-batch re-repair proof. One must prove that all effective
   671	leases at the new frame are already compatible with one supported
   672	proposal path, and that the pending gate and later re-entry preserve
   673	that fact. Assuming the first unanimous batch to prove that batch is
   674	circular. The stronger bound is **OPEN**; Opus's argument is **SKETCH**.
   675	No impossibility of a better bound is asserted.
