    80	
    81	**B01. Certificate meanings must be shared.** `Cert` chooses its own facets. A profile is only a set of claim IDs. Thus one protocol can certify safety using an output that always returns genesis, then certify inclusion using a different getter under the same output name. For example, an output can show each of two conflicting honest proposals at its inclusion deadline. It can satisfy inclusion while failing safety; the genesis getter supplies the separate safety certificate. The current bundle instead uses one `I` and one `C` throughout. Profiles need a common instance interface, consistent parameter bindings, and explicit facet equality or refinement proofs. Cross-claim inequalities alone do not fix this. [Design:344](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:344), [current bundle:23](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Claims.lean:23).
    82	
    83	**B02. Delay weakening is false for independent inclusion claims.** `IncludedFrom … D` requires containment at exactly `proposalTime + D`. It does not require containment at later reads. An output can contain B at `s + D`, then remove B before `s + D′`. This refutes the proposed generic implication for `D ≤ D′`. All current inclusion fields have this problem when separated from their safety fields. The complete bundles can supply monotonicity, but partial certification cannot assume that other certificate. Preserve the old exact-delay entry and use a relation theorem that requires the same output’s monotonicity certificate. Alternatively, introduce a new entry with persistent inclusion. [Properties:89](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Properties.lean:89), [design:286](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:286).
    84	
    85	**B03. One witness does not prevent vacuity.** Consider a protocol that emits a valid heartbeat and an undeliverable object at time zero. Give the second object `depsPresent = false`, `excludes = false`, and no alternative handler. These hooks satisfy the listed monotonicity obligations. A short run can deliver the heartbeat and satisfy the proposed witness. However, once the broadcast deadline enters the horizon, synchrony requires a delivery that `DeliveryWellFormed` forbids. No longer synchronous run exists. Future inclusion obligations can therefore hold vacuously. The witness does not even depend on the conclusion delay. Require semantic links between hooks and execution, honest-emission admissibility, and feasibility beyond each relevant deadline—preferably a run-extension theorem. Also, `handles → equality ∨ carries` proves containment, not that the handler actually ran. [Design:133](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:133), [delivery checks:51](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Conditions.lean:51), [broadcast:110](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Conditions.lean:110).
    86	
    87	**B04. The anchor predicates need a new statement.** Under the natural compatibility interpretation, `Static` permits arbitrary anchor changes while there are no honest targets: its exception is then vacuous. HavocFG can keep changing the effective choice domain before a source forms. This is not the fixed-domain interval used by the research repair proof. Conversely, literal equality of anchor sets can be too strong: new branches can enter a view without changing the effective root or numerical band. Define the effective controls and the retained paths, with explicit interval endpoints, settling bounds, and quantifiers over duties. Define HavocFG as a family indexed by a signing rule; the AP facets and certificates must be uniform across that family. [Design:481](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:481), [design:501](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:501), [fixed-frontier proof:513](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:513).
    88	
    89	The Outage statement has a separate error. Persistence of an armed B does not imply that all later honest leases and stable tips remain on one chain. During a partition, nodes can adopt conflicting descendants X and Y of B while both retain B. The research proof protects **B**, using a first departure from B. It does not prove compatibility of every new protected descendant. Consequently, (C) cannot generally maintain the proposed global `ProtectedCompatible` predicate throughout Outage. Use a predicate indexed by the protected read or armed prefix. State same-round target compatibility only in regimes that establish it. [Design:507](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:507), [Outage proof:380](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:380).
    90	
    91	**B05. Simplex does not satisfy the stated row premise.** A height pair names the immutable height entry of its source C; that entry need not equal C. A finality pair names the head-derived J. It can be nonempty when `FGTarget` is empty. The design correctly introduces `FinalityHead`, then incorrectly requires every honest row to name the AP target or be empty. Split the proof into height-source provenance, entry extraction, and finality provenance through an earlier justification quorum. Keep the source **body** in the proof history. The certified-pair rule supports the first NJ+1 transfer, but does not by itself prove the later history required by (F). [Design:559](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:559), [DC client:2139](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:2139), [source-history requirement:417](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:417).
    92	
    93	**B06. Joint recovery has missing interface obligations.** Safe, advancing FG targets do not imply recovery of every exposed AP output. An AP can retain the required target behavior while a separate output chooses conflicting descendants of F after an asynchronous prefix. `ap.floored` still holds. `ap.cleanCompatible` says nothing about this recovery execution. Add conditional recovery claims for the ordinary outputs, including their relationship to targets and the handoff state.
    94	
    95	Finality also needs more than a head that descends the chosen anchor. The adjudication still requires the correct current-slot finality head, a usable non-NJ height, record permission, and timed carriers under strong proposer recurrence. Full participation alone supplies none of those proposer windows. The design must expose these requirements rather than hide them in “FG liveness premises.” [Design:517](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:517), [remaining finality obligations:670](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:670), [DC FinalityRegime:126](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/decoupled-consensus/verified-consensus/DecoupledConsensusStatements/Generic/Regimes.lean:126).
    96	
    97	**M01. The engine needs a stated scope.** E1 and E2 support many finite scheduled-duty models. They do not establish the broader claim that every later key or eligibility scheme is an L1–L3 addition. Honest code still has no private key or random input; each label has a fixed act time and seat count. Genuine private VRF selection and drifted execution of one logical duty require more than a participation predicate. The feature table below distinguishes direct extensions from restricted encodings. [Design:88](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:88), [current duty interface:55](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainModel/Generic/Run.lean:55).
    98	
    99	**M02. E5 and E6 need executable semantics before the lift is frozen.** Today `World.step` has no environment or seat assignment argument. Recording emitted signatures makes the state transition depend on both. Specify whether all duty payloads use the same pre-record state, how records are folded, and how restart is ordered against ticks and deliveries. DC records before release. Moreover, resetting the base loses its accepted F; finalized monotonicity then fails across restart unless F is durable or the claim explicitly excludes the restart/rejoin interval. The isolation equations are sound for local reads, but an AP projection also needs laws for proposal emissions and gossip, which are not covered by “every C* act.” [Run:105](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainModel/Generic/Run.lean:105), [design:175](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:175), [DC record-before-release:2188](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:2188).
   100	
   101	**M03. The lift is feasible, but the stated theorem is insufficient.** Use a disjoint label sum for old committee labels and proposal slots. Translate proposer authentication to seat zero of the corresponding singleton label. Recover `proposerOf s` from that seat’s holder. Recover each old seat equivalence from a proved bijection—not merely a seat-count equality. Preserve the order of duties and the tick-before-delivery rule. Translate outer objects and the `carries`/`handles` relations, including carried signatures. No self-visible labels and `NoRestart` then permit a state-fold simulation. Claim transport additionally needs emission, holder, proposal, root-scope, and regime equivalences. Equal output reads alone cannot transfer `HonestProposalAt`. [Design:206](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:206), [proposal predicate:58](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Properties.lean:58).
   102	
   103	Every current claim can plausibly transfer through those adapters, including claims that quantify over `proposerOf`. Existing proof files can remain unchanged if the old engine remains available and new wrapper theorems transport them. This is not automatic reuse against renamed definitions. My planning estimate is **several engineer-weeks**, with medium confidence; proof-interface compatibility is likely to cost more than the event-fold induction.
   104	
   105	**M04. The anchor representation needs a proved correspondence.** DC’s viable set includes processed ancestors and descendants, not just an antichain of entries. An anchor set can represent the permitted choice cones only together with the duty view, ancestry, admission rules, and root convention. Root = J gives `{J}` under the required entry invariants; that special case is reasonable. However, R1 recomputes control values from the duty view, while DC uses live control caches with an explicit processed-block view. The research calls R1 an explicit change. Preserve the accepted F and its basis across the F-advance cut, and specify how frozen branches contribute to maxima. Also distinguish finality-before-height in the **chain transition** from the store update, which updates `h_max`, then J, then F. [Design:452](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:452), [DC viable tree:911](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:911), [DC store update:937](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/healing/rewrite/consensus.tex:937), [R1 change:132](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-fast.md:132).
   106	
   107	**M05. The proposed induction needs causal prefix statements.** Several regimes impose a minimum horizon, so they are not closed under arbitrary truncation with parameters held fixed. Also, a theorem conditional on `TargetPreserving` for an entire future interval cannot establish that same condition by merely invoking “induction on time.” State the induction invariant and a step lemma that uses only earlier sources, receipts, and duties. The research supplies the required ordering: the new row at the action cannot justify the earlier transfer HEAD. Outage has no `gst_zero` field; its clean history must follow from its healthy-prefix conditions. [Design:594](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md:594), [causal ordering:279](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/fgsa-adjudicate.md:279).
